 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The transformation involves matrix multiplications ($3 \times 3$) and vector operations, which are $O(1)$ per kernel. However, it is applied only every 5000 iterations, minimizing the overhead per frame [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** No additional significant memory is required beyond the standard Gaussian attributes, as the transformation is computed on-the-fly or stored as a modified covariance [S1].
-- **Performance:** On the Mip-NeRF 360 dataset, 3D-HGS with an efficient rasterizer achieves 125 FPS and 29.66 PSNR using 3.8M kernels, compared to 3DGS which uses 3.2M kernels [S1]. On Tank & Dengin, it achieves 160 FPS and 24.45 PSNR with 1.8M kernels [S1]. On Tank & Blending, it achieves 126 FPS and 29.76 PSNR with 2.6M kernels [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Consistently outperforms original 3D Gaussian Splatting across all metrics (PSNR, etc.) on evaluated datasets [S1]. The half-ellipsoid shape effectively models sharp transitions between object surfaces and empty space, reducing noise [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Requires accurate surface normal estimation to define the cutting plane; if normals are noisy, the half-Gaussian orientation may be incorrect. The method increases the number of kernels compared to standard 3DGS in some datasets (e.g., 3.8M vs 3.2M on Mip-NeRF 360) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity**: The transformation involves matrix multiplications ($3 \times 3$) and vector operations, which is $O(1)$ per Gaussian. However, it is only applied every 5000 iterations, making the per-step overhead negligible.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: No additional memory is required beyond the standard Gaussian parameters.
+*   **Performance**:
+    *   On the Mip-NeRF 360 dataset, 3D-HGS with an Efficient Rasterizer achieves 125 FPS and 29.66 PSNR using 3.8M kernels, compared to 3DGS which uses 3.2M kernels [S1].
+    *   On the Tank & Dengin dataset, 3D-HGS achieves 160 FPS and 24.45 PSNR using 1.8M kernels [S1].
+    *   On the Tank & Blending dataset, 3D-HGS achieves 126 FPS and 29.76 PSNR using 2.6M kernels [S1].
+*   **Strengths**: Consistently outperforms original 3D Gaussian Splatting across all metrics (PSNR, etc.) on evaluated datasets [S1]. The splitting planes align well with scene surface normals, effectively modeling sharp transitions [S1].
+*   **Limitations**: Requires accurate surface normal estimation. The method increases the number of kernels compared to standard 3DGS in some datasets (e.g., 3.8M vs 3.2M on Mip-NeRF 360) to achieve higher quality.
+*   **When to use**: When volumetric artifacts and "blobby" geometry are problematic, and surface normals are available or can be estimated.
+*   **When not to use**: If computational resources are extremely limited and the slight PSNR gain does not justify the increased kernel count or complexity.
 
 ## Background
-3D Half-Gaussian Splatting (3D-HGS) was introduced in the paper "3D-HGS: 3D Half-Gaussian Splatting" [S1]. The method addresses the limitation of full ellipsoids in representing sharp boundaries by cutting the Gaussian in half along the surface normal. It is evaluated against architectures such as Scaffold, mip-splatting, and MCMC [S1]. The approach is part of the broader family of regularization and geometry constraints in Gaussian Splatting, specifically focusing on spatial/shape domain modifications [S1].
+3D Half-Gaussian Splatting (3D-HGS) was introduced in the paper "3D-HGS: 3D Half-Gaussian Splatting" [S1]. The method builds upon the differentiable rendering framework of 3D Gaussian Splatting. It is evaluated against architectures such as Scaffold, mip-splatting, and MCMC [S1]. The core innovation is the use of half-Gaussians to better represent surface geometry, reducing the need for excessive Gaussian density to approximate sharp edges.
 
